\section{Source and cleaning}{

\code{reefs} is a cleaned copy of what the FWC feature service returns
rather than a reworking of it. All 4,548 deployments are kept and no
row is filtered. The build script is \code{data-raw/reefs.R}, which is not
installed with the package; the download and the cleaning it calls are
the same internal code \code{\link[=fetch_reefs]{fetch_reefs()}} uses, so the snapshot and a
live fetch cannot drift apart.

The service publishes these fields: \code{DeployID}, \code{County}, \code{DDate},
\code{DeployDate}, \code{Description}, \code{Name}, \code{MatDescrip}, \code{MatCat},
\code{Tonnage}, \code{Relief}, \code{Depth}, \code{Jurisdiction}, \code{Coast}, \code{Lat_DM},
\code{Long_DM}, \code{Lat_DD}, \code{Long_DD}, and \code{LocAccuracy}, alongside internal
identifiers and geometry that are not carried over.

Four changes are applied:
\enumerate{
\item Field names are converted to snake_case, and a few are
renamed for clarity: \code{Name} becomes \code{deployment_name},
\code{MatDescrip} becomes \code{primary_material}, \code{MatCat} becomes
\code{material_category}, and \code{Tonnage} becomes \code{tons}.
\item \code{DDate}, an integer of the form \code{yyyymmdd} that uses \code{00} and
\code{99} as sentinels for unknown parts, becomes \code{deploy_date} plus
\code{date_precision}, \code{deploy_year}, \code{deploy_month}, and
\code{deploy_before}.
\item A recorded tonnage of 0 becomes \code{NA}, affecting 1,162 rows.
\item \code{LocAccuracy}, coded 1 to 3, is spelled out as \code{"Low"},
\code{"Medium"}, and \code{"High"}, matching FWC's own printed lists.
}

Rows are sorted by \code{deploy_id}. Nothing else is touched:
\code{primary_material} and the degrees-and-minutes coordinate strings are
left exactly as the service returns them.
}

\section{Comparison with the published Excel list}{

FWC also distributes an Excel list of the same deployments. The two
agree on every deployment, on all coordinates, and on tonnage, relief,
depth, county, coast, jurisdiction, and accuracy. They differ in three
small ways, all of which favour the service:

\itemize{
\item The service keeps inch marks that the spreadsheet drops, so a
material reads \verb{Piling 3-6" X 12" X 12"} rather than
\verb{Piling 3-6' X 12 X 12}. This affects 24 rows.
\item The service carries \code{material_category}, which the
spreadsheet has no equivalent for.
\item The spreadsheet records 17 early deployments as \code{Pre-1970},
where the service gives no date at all. Those rows appear here
with \code{date_precision} of \code{"unknown"}. This is the one respect in
which the spreadsheet holds more information.
}
}

